ease affecting most of the tissues of the body, characterized by the formation of tubercles and the presence in the diseased parts of the tubercle-bacillus.—{{smaller|'''Acute miliary tuberculosis''', an acute affection characterized by the deposit of large numbers of minute tubercules in various organs of the body, accompanied by high fever, rapid pulse, and marked prostration; galloping or quick consumption. The disease is almost always rapidly fatal.—'''Laryngeal tuberculosis.''' Same as ''tubercular laryngitis'' (which see, under ''tubercular'').—'''Pulmonary tuberculosis''', tuberculosis of the lungs, popularly called consumption.}}

'''tuberculous''' (tū-bėr′kū-lus), ''a.'' [= F. ''tuberculeux'' = Sp. Pg. ''tuberculoso'' = It. ''tubercoloso'', < ML. *''tuberculosus'', < L. ''tuberculum'', a tubercle: see ''tubercle''.] '''1.''' Tubercular; tuberculate.—'''2.''' In ''pathol.'', affected by tubercles; exhibiting or containing tubercles.—'''3.''' Pertaining to or of the nature of tuberculosis.
{{smaller block|Greek elephantiasis . . . is a ''tuberculous'' disease affecting especially the skin, the mouth, and the nasal fossæ, and the organs of voice and respiration.
{{right|''J. M. Carnochan'', Operative Surgery, p. 39.}}}}

